-- The counts card shipped in #94 windows plus or minus four around the count
-- step 4 published. Step 4 publishes one end of a BAND, so the card never
-- reaches the other end -- found by looking at the real calculator rather
-- than the seeded fixture the tests use.
--
-- Written as the full description rather than an append: the migration and
-- seed.sql are compared as literal end states, so a concatenation cannot be
-- checked.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set description = 'THE FINDING THAT MOTIVATES THIS, from the 2026-09-10 panel-guidance session: PanelSpec in src/lib/pv-string.ts has TEN FIELDS AND EVERY ONE OF THEM IS A VOLT OR AN AMP. No length, no width, no mass, no module efficiency, no cell technology, no degradation rate. That is precisely why the site could not answer "why 3 x 400 W rather than 2 x 600 W" until /guides/choosing-panels hand-wrote the physical numbers as class-typical prose with a caveat attached. Widening the model is what makes the whole question computable instead of editorial. FIELDS TO ADD: lengthMm, widthMm, weightKg, moduleEfficiency, cellTech (perc | topcon | hjt), bifacial, degradationYear1, degradationAnnual, warrantyYears, connector. A FOURTH CROSS-CHECK COMES FREE in panel-review.ts, in the same physics-shaped family as the existing fill factor test: module efficiency must equal Pmax / (area x 1000 W/m2), which catches dimensions read off the wrong row without needing any new source of truth. Weight per square metre is a second: glass-backsheet lands near 11-13 kg/m2, glass-glass near 15-17. ITEM (1) SHIPPED 2026-09-10 as src/lib/panel-granularity.ts and the counts table on /calculators/strings. IT SHIPPED AS COUNT GRANULARITY, NOT A WATTAGE SURVEY, and the reason is a gate, not an oversight: evaluating a wattage needs that panel''s Voc, Isc and coefficients, and PANEL_PRESETS holds exactly ONE admitted panel (the SG550WM) plus two modules labelled on their face as made up. A wattage survey across one real product is not a survey. The wattage half is therefore gated on panel_models, which is deliberately sequenced after the battery pipeline -- so what shipped is the half that is honest with the data actually in the repo, and it is the half that resolves the flagship disagreement. WHAT IT DOES: for the chosen panel and tracker it scans a range of panel COUNTS, reports which can be wired into whole strings at all, the nearest working count above and below the energy target, how far each lands from that target, and how many ways each count factors. A CORRECTNESS BUG WAS FOUND AND FIXED doing it. When nothing fitted, /calculators/strings told the reader "a different panel, a bigger unit, or splitting the array across more trackers are the ways out; A BIGGER ARRAY ON THE SAME BOX IS NOT." For the site''s own admitted pairing that is FALSE: SG550WM + SPH10048P at -25.9 degC has no safe arrangement of 11 panels and wires 12 cleanly as 6S2P, on the same box. The page now names the nearest counts that work instead of steering people away from the fix, and a test locks the claim to the datasheets so it cannot come back. THREE FACTS THE SCAN TURNED UP, none of which the one-count view could see. (a) IT IS NOT A PARITY RULE. The wirable set is 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 -- "odd counts fail" only reads true if you start counting at nine. Seven wires fine as a single string of seven. panel-granularity.ts reports the SET and offers parity only as an observation about the window it examined, with a counterexample required before it will claim one; for this pairing it correctly stays silent. (b) THERE IS A CEILING: above 16 panels nothing fits on this unit whatever it factors into, which is 8.8 kW of panel against an 11 kW input -- a real limit no single-count page can show. (c) THE REAL MECHANISM IS FACTORS, NOT ODDNESS. A count can only be wired the ways it divides, so a PRIME count has just two: one tall string or one wide row. Eleven is too tall for the 500 V input one way and too wide for the 22 A tracker the other, with nothing in between; twelve factors six ways and one of them fits. That is now the teaching line under the table, and "ways to wire" is a column. REMAINING, and all of it needs the physical fields: (2) a FORM-FACTOR AND HANDLING panel on /calculators/panels -- array area, per-panel weight, a one-person-lift flag, total footprint, longest dimension against a stated mounting limit. Pairs directly with "Ask installation environment up front" -- cabin / boat / camper / shed IS the input that decides form factor. (3) a SIDE-BY-SIDE BUILD COMPARISON (3 x 400 against 2 x 600) showing area, weight, valid arrangements, shade exposure and delivered cost; also the natural affiliate surface, since panels are the largest line item after the battery. (4) a DERIVED FREIGHT-CLASS field: longest dimension over about 2 m means LTL rather than parcel. Cheap to compute, deliberately NOT scraped. SEQUENCING, decided 2026-09-10 and unchanged: the panel_models table waits for the battery pipeline. Ship one pipeline before starting the second. NEXT, AND IT IS SMALL -- found 2026-09-11 by Vincent looking at the real step 4 rather than a seeded one. The panel step does not publish a single count, it shows a BAND (6-16 in the case seen: the annual-average figure at one end, the worst month at the other) and publishes only panelsAnnual -- whichever end the user has currently selected via the peak-sun input and the "Size against <month>" button. That publishing is correct. What is wrong is the counts card downstream: windowAround() spans plus or minus four around the selected count, so sitting at 6 it shows roughly 2-10 and NEVER REACHES THE TOP OF THE BAND. Someone weighing a summer array against a December array gets no wiring information about half the range they are actually choosing between -- which is the decision step 4 exists to put in front of them. FIX: carry the band on PanelSummary (panelsWorst alongside panels; panelCountBand already computes it in the panels page and it is simply thrown away) and have the counts card span the band when it is wider than the default window. The test that missed this seeded a single count of 11, so add one that seeds a band.'
  where title = 'Panel choice: form factor, granularity and the physical fields';
